Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 15:09:27 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 077/154] feat(chrome): glow the window with the colours of the
 page's own edges
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The chrome carried one flat colour, sampled from a declared theme-color.
Most pages declare none, so it never moved. When it did move, the bound
was applied to the value rather than to the screen: the window paints
that value at the glass alpha, so at the shipped 0.2 a 22 percent shift
arrived as about 4 percent, which is one to ten RGB units.

The window now reads its own viewport eight times a second, downscaled
to 24x24 on the GPU before the readback so a tick moves 2.3KB rather
than megabytes. Four edge means come off that one copy, each resolved
separately so the bound and the contrast floor apply per edge, and they
are painted as gradients out of the page card into the chrome.

The rate is a ladder - 125ms, 500ms, 2000ms - stepping down after eight
unchanged reads and snapping back the moment anything moves. A hidden or
occluded tab is never read. An unchanged read paints nothing.

An error page is never sampled: it is Chromium's own chrome in the
site's clothes, and it declares white, so every failed load would wash
the window the same way. That rule gates both the readback and the
declared-tag path - gating only the first still let the white through.

A tab already loaded commits nothing when it comes to the front, so the
sampler runs on an active-tab change too. Without it a reload was the
only gesture that moved the chrome off the last page to navigate.

ABI 39 -> 40: atrium_core_page_tint takes the glass alpha, because the
bound and the contrast floor now grade the composited colour.

Signed-off-by: János Mikó <693558+janosmiko@users.noreply.github.com>
